{"id": 1929777, "name": "HAMPTON v. HUGHES", "name_abbreviation": "Hampton v. Hughes", "decision_date": "1910-03-22", "docket_number": "7516", "first_page": "343", "last_page": "344", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "85 S.C. 343"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [{"cite": "69 S. C., 520", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4422394], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/69/0520-01"]}, {"cite": "73 S. C., 33", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "83 S. C., 1", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [1926401], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/83/0001-01"]}, {"cite": "78 S. C., 403", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [1917236], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/78/0403-01"]}, {"cite": "73 S. C., 292", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [2351227], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/73/0292-01"]}, {"cite": "71 S. C., 404", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4425602], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/71/0404-01"]}, {"cite": "69 S. C., 630", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "63 S. C., 22", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [2350721], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/63/0022-01"]}, {"cite": "1 Mill., 166", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Mill", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "29 S. C., 388", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "8 Rich., 132", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Rich.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "36 S. C., 351", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "53 S. E., 481", "category": "reporters:state_regional", "reporter": "S.E.", "opinion_index": 0}, {"cite": "73 S. C., 292", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [2351227], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/73/0292-01"]}], "analysis": {"cardinality": 206, "char_count": 2477, "ocr_confidence": 0.573, "pagerank": {"raw": 7.259244762335987e-08, "percentile": 0.43269935814790744}, "sha256": "82f3650d997cbcefa3cb509252b995fe27590363d6e2df8e9475192b5eb2f31d", "simhash": "1:5cfed2f2b3429217", "word_count": 423}, "last_updated": "2024-02-27T19:00:45.680843+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["HAMPTON v. HUGHES."], "opinions": [{"text": "The opinion of the Court was delivered by\nMr. Justice Hydrick.\nThis was an action in the court of a magistrate to recover a mare. Over defendant\u2019s objection, a printed paper, signed by plaintiff and the sheriff of Buncombe County, N. C., containing a description of the mare, and offering a reward of fifty dollars for her, was admitted.\nPlaintiff had a verdict and judgment, which was affirmed on appeal to the Circuit Court.\nThe exception that the printed reward was improperly admitted in evidence cannot be sustained, because no ground of objection to it was stated at the trial in the magistrate\u2019s court, and because everything it contained was testified to by one or more of -the witnesses without objection. The admission of the paper itself was, therefore, harmless, if error, and the exception is technical. Section 368 of the Code, relative to appeals from inferior courts to the Circuit Court provides: \u201cUpon hearing the appeal, the appellate court shall give judgment according to the justice of the case, without regard to technical errors and defects which do not affect the merits.\u201d Jenkins v. Ry., 73 S. C., 292, 53 S. E., 481.\nThe other exceptions involve questions of fact which cannot be reviewed by this Court. Judgment affirmed.", "type": "majority", "author": "Mr. Justice Hydrick."}], "attorneys": ["Mr. Adam C. Welborn, for appellant,", "Messrs. Hodges & Daniel, contra,"], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "7516\nHAMPTON v. HUGHES.\n1. Evidence \u2014 Magistrate Court \u2014 Appeal.\u2014Admission of printed reward for return of a horse in claim and delivery is harmless, if error here, because no objection was made to it when offered and its contents was testified to by other witnesses without objection. The objection is also technical, which objections are disregarded by the Circuit Court on appeal from magistrate court.\n2. Appeal. \u2014 Exceptions involving questions of fact on appeal from circuit judgments in appeal from magistrate\u2019s courts will not be considered here.\nBefore C. C. FeaithERSTOnE, Special Judge, Greenville, June, 1909.\nAffirmed.\nAction by Wade Hampton against E. C. Hughes. From circuit order affirming judgment of Magistrate E. Inman, defendant appeals.\nMr. Adam C. Welborn, for appellant,\ncites : An advertisement is hearsay: 16 Cyc., 1213-4; 36 S. C., 351; 8 Rich., 132; 29 S. C., 388; Dud., 181; 1 Mill., 166.\nMessrs. Hodges & Daniel, contra,\ncite: Questions of fact decided below will not be reviewed here: 63 S. C., 22; 67 S. C\u201e 35; 69 S. C., 630; 70' S. C., 554, 178; 71 S. C., 404; 73 S. C., 292; 78 S. C., 403 ; 83 S. C., 1. Jurisdiction of magistrate: 73 S. C., 33; 69 S. C., 520; Art. V, Sec. 21.\nMarch 22, 1910."}, "file_name": "0343-01", "first_page_order": 359, "last_page_order": 360}